  "historical_output_text": " Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy stored in sugar molecules. This process is central to life on Earth as it provides the primary energy source for nearly all ecosystems and oxygen for us to breathe.\n\nThe main idea of photosynthesis is that green plants capture sunlight, water and carbon dioxide (CO2) from the air and use this as raw materials to make sugar and oxygen. Here's a simplified overview of the sequence:\n\n1. Light absorption: Chlorophyll, a green pigment in plant cells, absorbs sunlight. This energy excites electrons in the chlorophyll molecules.\n\n2. Electron transport: The excited electrons are passed along a series of proteins and molecules that act like an electron \"relay race\" inside the plant cell. This generates a proton gradient that powers ATP and NADPH production.\n\n3. Carbon fixation: The plant uses the ATP",
